<PolicyLayout
  title="Policy for Safety of Vulnerable Persons"
  subtitle="Public Governance Statement & Safeguarding Framework"
  description="How Empower Kiribati safeguards children, youth, women, the elderly, people with disabilities, and minority groups across all of its activities and programs."
>
  <p>
    This policy covers our engagement with vulnerable individuals, including children, youth, women,
    the elderly, people with disabilities, and minority groups. It applies to all partners, role
    fillers, contractors, and volunteers performing accountabilities on our behalf, and is held by
    our Guidance Circle.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Purpose &amp; Organizational Commitment</h2>
  <p>
    We are committed to being an organization that promotes the welfare of all vulnerable persons,
    including children and at-risk community groups. We recognize our primary responsibility to
    promote safe practices that safeguard vulnerable persons from all forms of abuse and exploitation
    across all operations, activities, and programs.
  </p>
  <p>
    All personnel working within our office, activities, and programs actively work together to
    embrace difference and diversity, uphold human rights, and ensure a safe, supportive environment
    for all.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Core Principles</h2>
  <p>We operate under four foundational safeguarding principles:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Right to Safety and Well-Being:</strong> We recognize that children, youth, women, persons
      with disabilities, and other minority groups represent vulnerable populations who have an
      absolute right to feel and be safe.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Inclusive &amp; Empowering Environment:</strong> Our safe practices are designed to help
      vulnerable individuals build on their strengths and enhance their resilience through activities
      that are inclusive, meaningful, safe, and empowering.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Voice &amp; Leadership Development:</strong> We actively foster opportunities for
      vulnerable individuals to develop their voice and leadership within programs and community
      spaces.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Respect for Diversity &amp; Cultural Responsiveness:</strong> We implement safeguarding
      measures in a culturally sensitive and responsive manner, ensuring non-discrimination and equal
      access to educational and learning opportunities.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. Operational Safeguards &amp; Interaction Standards</h2>
  <p>
    To protect both vulnerable individuals and our role fillers during face-to-face engagements, our
    personnel must adhere to the following mandatory standards:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Accountable Decision-Making:</strong> No role filler shall make significant decisions
      regarding a vulnerable person's welfare without documented concurrence from at least one other
      role filler or a designated community partner, unless the individual is in immediate physical
      danger.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Culturally &amp; Demographically Sensitive Engagement:</strong> To minimize risks of abuse
      or exploitation and build trust, our engagements involve an appropriate person of the same
      gender, ethnicity, or cultural background as the vulnerable individual whenever possible.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Referral to Specialist Support Agencies:</strong> Any specific request for specialized
      assistance we receive from a vulnerable person must be promptly referred to the appropriate
      partner agency (such as the Women's Crisis Centre or Child Welfare) for professional support.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>4. Mandatory Reporting &amp; Intervention Protocols</h2>
  <p>We enforce an immediate and transparent reporting pathway for any safeguarding concerns:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Duty to Report:</strong> All of our partners, contractors, and volunteers operate under a
      strict "Duty to Report".
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>2-Hour Action Requirement:</strong> If any of our personnel senses a tension or concern
      regarding the safety or well-being of a vulnerable person, they must bypass our standard meeting
      cycles and report directly to the Compliance Lead within 2 hours.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>External Partner Referral:</strong> Upon receiving a report, the Compliance Lead will
      refer the matter to appropriate professional partner agencies or statutory authorities wherever
      possible to ensure timely protection.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>5. Data Privacy &amp; Encrypted Records</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Restricted Data Handling:</strong> Identifiable sensitive information regarding
      vulnerable individuals is strictly excluded from our standard transparency practices.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Encrypted Storage:</strong> Records containing sensitive information must be stored in an
      Encrypted Domain.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Collection Limits:</strong> Data regarding persons under 18 or those lacking legal capacity
      is collected strictly for educational and program delivery purposes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>System Alignment:</strong> All digital data collection and storage must fully align with
      our Data Security &amp; Cybersafety Policy.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>6. Legal Framework &amp; Regulatory Alignment</h2>
  <p>
    This policy ensures all of our operations align with national legislation, community standards,
    and international conventions relating to human rights and child protection. Key reference
    legislation and frameworks include:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Children, Young People, and Family Welfare Act (2013)</li>
    <li>Te Rau N Te Mwenga Act (2014)</li>
    <li>Inclusive Education Policy (2015)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Additionally, active community and agency partnerships are required across all monitoring and
    review phases for programs involving vulnerable groups to maintain ongoing compliance and local
    alignment.
  </p>
</PolicyLayout>
